spoint veg perf CORRECTION (supersedes the 'fragment-bound' claim in veg-rocks-perf-run-2026-06-18, which was WRONG). Decisive in-browser experiments (settled scene, AMD Radeon iGPU): (1) PIXEL-RATIO SWEEP is FLAT - fps 27.8 at pixelRatio 0.3 vs 22.2 at 1.5 (25x pixel difference) -> the forest is NOT fragment-bound. (2) FRAME DECOMPOSITION at DPR 1.0: baseline veg+rocks+shadow 24.8fps/40ms; veg-hidden 54.1fps (veg COLOR pass ~22ms); sun-shadow-off 50.8fps (SHADOW pass ~20ms); rocks ~2ms only. (3) VEG SUB-DECOMP: branches-hidden 51.5fps -> BRANCHES cost ~21ms for only 112 VISIBLE trees (ez-tree branch geometry ~10k tris each); leaves ~4.6ms. So the bottleneck is GEOMETRY/VERTEX THROUGHPUT (high-poly ez-tree branches), not fragment, not CPU (update 0.5ms), not draw-calls (48 draws). The last-session '2x from DPR+cheap-shadows' was almost ALL the cheap-shadows/trunk-only-shadow (shadow pass is geometry-heavy); DPR/resolution is nearly free here. THE FIX = geometry reduction: aggressive mesh LOD (pull D1/D2/D3 cutovers IN + lower simplify ratios) cut tris 891k->43k(forced)/186k(shipped) and lifted fps 24.8->34.9. The earlier 'impostor pushes fps to 5' was a BAKE-STALL measurement artifact (impostors not settled), NOT a real impostor regression - impostor-heavy settled = 34.9fps fine. CRITICAL MEASUREMENT RULE: only measure steady fps AFTER window.__vegProfile.impostors===5 AND totalInstances stable (bakes stall multi-second). Live experiment helpers: window.__dprOff=true then window.__app.renderer.setPixelRatio(p); toggle window.__veg._meshes[].branch/leaf.visible + window.__rocks._meshes[].im.visible + sun.castShadow; mutate mesh.LODinfo.render.levels[i].distance (SQUARED metres) to force LOD live. Also: this iGPU's PLANET BACKDROP (tv8 mapspinner GPU render) caps ~58-60fps even with zero veg+no shadow+low res -> 144fps-on-THIS-iGPU is gated by the base scene; the veg work still matters for slower/faster devices + headroom. streaming-gltf GPU-only render was investigated + DECLINED (its GPU instance-texture/multi-draw/vertex-cull are CPU/draw-call wins, don't reduce vertex throughput; per-asset streamer not a mass instancer).